<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/advanced/ethnographic-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ethnography is anthropology's signature method: <strong>deep immersion to understand a social
    world from inside it</strong> — the discipline's answer to "how can you know another life?"
    Its rigour is unusual: a year in the field beats a thousand quick surveys for depth, at the
    cost of scale, and its techniques have spread to design, medicine and tech research.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the contract, the lenses, the ethics" widget="equationStep" config={ethnCfg} />

  <h2>Where ethnography went to work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corporate ethnography:</strong> Intel, Microsoft and IDEO employ anthropologists — watching real behaviour beats asking users what they want (the design lesson's UX research owes the method directly).</li>
    <li><strong>Medicine:</strong> Arthur Kleinman showed illness as experience, not just pathology — medical anthropology reshaped how clinics understand chronic disease, pain and adherence.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital ethnography:</strong> communities online studied with the same immersion (games, forums, platforms) — the method transfers; the field site became partly virtual.</li>
    <li><strong>The honest limits:</strong> small n, possible unrepresentativeness, observer effects — which is why ethnography pairs with surveys, demography and history rather than standing alone (the methods lesson of every social science in this atlas).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Malinowski, Argonauts of the Western Pacific (1922); Geertz, The Interpretation of Cultures (1973); Kleinman, The Illness Narratives (1988); Boellstorff on digital ethnography.</p>
</TopicPage>
